WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:04.980
Samsung has just announced its Galaxy S10
line of smartphones, and the S10+ model is

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:06.190
looking really impressive.

00:00:06.190 --> 00:00:10.339
Let’s talk about the 5 features that are
a step ahead of Apple’s iPhone XS.

00:00:10.339 --> 00:00:13.549
For starters, the new Infinity O display is
a game-changer.

00:00:13.549 --> 00:00:17.150
It’s very color accurate and incredibly
bright.

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:22.400
DisplayMate measured a peak brightness of
up to 1200 nits compared to 725 on the iPhone

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:23.400
XS Max.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:27.090
That’s actually a pretty massive difference,
making the S10’s display the brightest in

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:28.150
the industry.

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:33.480
This also helps it achieve HDR10+ support,
which is more widely supported than the iPhone

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:37.760
XS’s Dolby Vision support since HDR10+ is
royalty-free HDR10+ is royalty-free for video

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:39.179
production companies.

00:00:39.179 --> 00:00:44.059
The S10’s display is also the most color
accurate display in any smartphone, with slightly

00:00:44.059 --> 00:00:48.219
better DCI-P3 color accuracy than Apple’s
iPhone XS Max.

00:00:48.219 --> 00:00:52.139
We haven’t yet seen the display in person,
but we’ll be comparing it to the XS Max

00:00:52.139 --> 00:00:53.820
as soon as we get our hands on it.

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:58.179
From the looks of it, Apple’s gonna have
to add ProMotion technology to their 2019

00:00:58.179 --> 00:01:01.379
iPhones if they want to top Samsung’s Infinity
O display.

00:01:01.379 --> 00:01:03.069
For the second feature, it’s the design.

00:01:03.069 --> 00:01:07.140
There were a few things that bugged me about
the older Samsung smartphone designs that

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:12.461
kept the iPhones on a step above Samsung,
but all of those things seem to finally be

00:01:12.461 --> 00:01:13.650
perfected with the S10.

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:18.280
First of all, the display is notchless, with
a hole punch camera cutout that’s cut using

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:21.280
a laser so the surrounding pixels aren’t
damaged.

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:25.210
I’ve got to admit that the cutout looks
great and it allows for a truly edge-to-edge

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:26.870
display that’s incredibly immersive.

00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:30.710
To make it even better, the fingerprint sensor
is finally gone from the back of the phone,

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:35.710
making it look incredibly clean, and the addition
of another lens makes the camera bump longer

00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:36.760
and more aesthetically pleasing.

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:37.760
Also, Samsung has finally removed the FCC
labeling near the bottom, and there’s no

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:38.760
Galaxy S10 label either, so the back now looks
very clean.

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:42.340
Even better, the S10+ gets two new ceramic
finishes that you can get if you pay for at

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:45.300
least 512GB of storage.

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:49.070
Ceramic is harder, tougher and more scratch-resistant
than glass.

00:01:49.070 --> 00:01:53.020
Although it’s harder, it can still crack
like grass, but maybe not as easily.

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:57.080
The ceramic model is also a bit heavier, so
it should make for a more premium feel.

00:01:57.080 --> 00:01:58.080
Fingerprints are harder to notice on Ceramic,
so that’s another bonus.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:00.640
Nonetheless, I still prefer the look of my
iPhone XS Max.

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:05.520
The design changes to the S10 just make it
look futuristic and modern compared to their

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:07.159
older Galaxy flagships.

00:02:07.159 --> 00:02:08.810
Now let’s talk about that fingerprint sensor.

00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:11.370
It’s under the display so it’s completely
hidden.

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:16.060
And the location is just perfect and so convenient
compared to having it on the back.

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:20.590
The best part about it is that it’s an ultrasonic
sensor instead of an optical sensor, which

00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:25.140
means that it actually uses bursts of sound
to map the fingerprint, which is similar to

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:29.310
the sonar waves that submarines use to map
objects underwater.

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:33.280
Optical sensors require the phone to blast
light onto your finger in order to read it.

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:37.380
The ultrasonic sensor doesn’t, so you can
actually unlock the S10 before you wake the

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:40.300
display, which is honestly my favorite part
about it.

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:44.730
And it should also be more accurate, reliable
and faster than optical sensors.

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:49.320
Apple is banking on Face ID, which works great,
but it does have it’s limitations, like

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:51.320
when it’s laying flat on a table.

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:55.650
The 2019 iPhone lineup is rumored to be getting
faster Face ID, but we’re not likely to

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:59.180
see an in-display fingerprint sensor until
least next year.

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:01.660
For the 4th feature, we’ve got the cameras.

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:06.209
Inside of the hole punch cutouts, we have
a 10-megapixel shooter in the S10 capable

00:03:06.209 --> 00:03:11.150
of shooting 4K video and a wide-angle 8MP
camera for depth sensing.

00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:16.270
The iPhone XS and XS Max feature a 7MP shooter
limited to 1080P 60.

00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:19.981
This should ultimately make for much better
selfie photos and video than with the iPhone

00:03:19.981 --> 00:03:22.900
XS, and exceptional depth sensing as well.

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:28.580
On the rear, the S10+ adds a 16MP ultra-wide
lens which makes for some pretty awesome-looking

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:31.530
photos with wide-lens distortion correction.

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:36.780
If you shoot 1080P video with the ultra wide
camera, you get some incredible video stabilization,

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:38.950
but it probably crops in a good amount.

00:03:38.950 --> 00:03:42.970
The best part about the camera is that it
shoots in the same HDR10+ format that the

00:03:42.970 --> 00:03:47.410
display supports, which is pretty unique since
even though the iPhone XS can shoot great

00:03:47.410 --> 00:03:51.500
HDR video, it still saves the file in standard
dynamic range.

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:54.959
We’ll have to see just how good the camera
is when we get our hands on it.

00:03:54.959 --> 00:03:55.959
g

00:03:55.959 --> 00:03:57.710
Finally, there’s the new Wireless PowerShare
feature.

00:03:57.710 --> 00:04:02.800
It basically allows your S10 to wirelessly
charge another Qi-compatible device, including

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:07.320
even their oldest wireless charging smartphones
and their new Galaxy Buds.

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:11.190
In terms of charging your friend’s smartphone,
it doesn’t seem very practical since it

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:15.540
renders your phone unusable while charging,
and you probably don’t want your own battery

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:19.220
to drain down to 30% just to charge their
phone.

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:23.120
Where it really makes sense is when you want
to charge your own wearable devices like any

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:28.900
of the Galaxy smartwatches, bands or most
likely, the new Galaxy Buds carrying case.

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:32.560
Since the batteries in those devices aren’t
very large, you don’t risk draining your

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:33.580
whole battery.

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:37.350
Now the most practical use-case for me is
that you can actually plug the S10 into the

00:04:37.350 --> 00:04:41.530
wall, and wirelessly charge another device
on top of it.

00:04:41.530 --> 00:04:45.330
Fast Charging 2.0 won’t work through the
wall due to heat concerns, but it’s still

00:04:45.330 --> 00:04:50.250
perfect for fully charging another device
and your S10 at the same time with only one

00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:51.250
charger.

00:04:51.250 --> 00:04:54.580
You can’t do this yet with any iPhone, but
there are rumors of Apple adding a similar

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:56.850
feature to their 2019 iPhone lineup.

00:04:56.850 --> 00:05:01.240
We’ll be getting our hands on the Galaxy
S10 as soon as it gets released, so make sure

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:03.100
to subscribe now so you don’t miss out on
those videos!

